    "rich_text_bj3bJ7": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_wjXC8n": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What Can You Make With DTF Transfers?",
            "heading_size": "h1"
          }
        },
        "text_9XK9jD": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>DTF transfers can be used for a wide range of custom apparel projects.</p><p>Popular applications include:</p><h3>T-Shirts</h3><p>From simple logos to large full-front artwork, DTF makes it easy to create colorful custom shirts.</p><h3>Hoodies & Sweatshirts</h3><p>Detailed graphics can be applied to the front, back, sleeves, or other suitable flat pressing areas.</p><h3>Tote Bags</h3><p>DTF is a useful option for branded merchandise, event bags, promotional products, and custom accessories.</p><h3>Workwear</h3><p>Businesses can use custom transfers for company logos, employee apparel, uniforms, and promotional clothing.</p><h3>Small Clothing Brands</h3><p>Instead of producing a large quantity of one design upfront, brands can order transfers and press garments according to demand.</p><p>That can make inventory planning more flexible.</p><h2>DTF vs. Screen Printing</h2><p>Both methods can produce excellent apparel, but they solve different production problems.</p><p><strong>DTF may be a better fit when you need:</strong></p><ul><li>Full-color artwork</li><li>Complex designs</li><li>Smaller quantities</li><li>Multiple designs</li><li>Fast design changes</li><li>No screen setup</li></ul><p><strong>Screen printing may make more sense when you need:</strong></p><ul><li>Very large quantities of the same design</li><li>Simple spot-color artwork</li><li>An established high-volume screen-printing workflow</li></ul><p>Neither method is automatically better for every project. The right choice depends on your artwork, quantity, garment, budget, and production setup.</p><h2>DTF vs. Sublimation</h2><p>The biggest difference is material compatibility.</p><p>Sublimation works by turning dye into gas and bonding it with compatible polyester fibers. It is therefore particularly well suited to light-colored polyester products.</p><p>DTF transfers create a printed and adhesive layer on the garment, giving them much broader flexibility across garment colors and fabric types.</p><p>For businesses that want to decorate cotton shirts, dark garments, polyester, and blends without relying on one specific fabric type, DTF can be an attractive option.</p><h2>DTF vs. Heat Transfer Vinyl</h2><p>Heat transfer vinyl (HTV) remains useful for names, numbers, lettering, and simple graphics.</p><p>But complex multicolor artwork can require several vinyl colors, cutting, alignment, and weeding.</p><p>With DTF, the complete artwork arrives as one ready-to-press transfer.</p><p>For detailed illustrations and full-color designs, this can save significant production time.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_wjXC8n",
        "text_9XK9jD"
      ],
